                <pb facs="0160" n="116" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v05p1/pg116"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>The information set forth above is derived from the despatch, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">infra</hi>, and from the following excerpt from a letter
                    of February 22, 1952, from Dorsz to William Rountree: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The same afternoon [February 20], Ed Martin asked me to seek Greek and
                            Turkish clearance on a draft paragraph to be added at the <pb
                                facs="0161" n="117" xml:id="pg117"/> end of the TCC resolution. The
                            text of this is being forwarded today by telegram. I got to the Greek
                            and Turkish Delegations just in time before the first NAC meeting and
                            handed a copy of the draft to Foreign Minister Koprulu and Mr.
                            Pipinelis. I asked both of them to look it over and suggested that they
                            might want me to drop around the following morning to discuss the
                            matter. That night I was asked to see Mr. Koprulu early yesterday
                            morning. The whole Turkish Delegation was there at the time and I was
                            asked to clarify many of the points which were troubling them. An
                            airgram which is shortly going out gives the highlights of this meeting
                            and will become available to the Department and to our Embassies in
                            Greece and Turkey.” (740.5/2–2252)</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">The airgram referred to by Dorsz is presumably the despatch
                    printed <hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi>. Regarding the other documents cited
                    by Dorsz, see footnotes 1 and 2, <hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi>.</p>
            </div>
